tion, the next read can retry. Good.\n\nBut nuance: If we use Promise.resolve().then(...).catch? We need ensure removal happens after promise settles. Since `entries.set(key, pending)` before await, and pending is a promise. We can attach `.finally(() => { if (entries.get(key) === pending) entries.delete(key); })`? Wait if success, we want keep the promise. So only on rejection we remove. Use `.catch((error) => { if (entries.get(key) === pending) entries.delete(key); throw error; })`. Since catch returns a new promise? It creates chained promise. But `pending` variable references the original load promise with catch attached. In entries, if we set pending to the result of chain, that's fine. We need compare entries.get(key) to the same object. But if we attach catch after assignment? Let's design:\n\n```\nlet pending = entries.get(key);\nif (!pending) {\n  const load = Promise.resolve().then(() => loadFromOrigin(key)).then(normalizeConfig);\n  pending = load.catch((error) => {\n    if (entries.get(key) === pending) entries.delete(key);\n    throw error;\n  });\n  entries.set(key, pending);\n}\nreturn copyConfig(await pending);\n```\n\nWait `pending` defined before assigned, used in catch closure. In JS, `pending` is captured; at catch execution, pending is the chain promise stored. However `entries.get(key)` returns `pending` (the catch chain), so condition true. If clear() called before failure: entries.delete(key). Then replacement call creates new pending2 and sets entries.set(key, pending2). When original load fails, `entries.get(key)` is pending2, not original pending; condition false, won't delete. Good.\n\nBut if success: load resolves normalized config, pending is fulfilled promise (from catch, since catch returns a promise that resolves to same value if no error). entries.get(key) === pending. We keep it. Good.\n\nHowever there is subtlety: In catch handler, if `entries.get(key) === pending` but `pending` is the chain promise set. If the entry was cleared and not replaced (entries.get returns undefined), condition false, no deletion. Fine.\n\nAlternative: store entry as object `{promise, generation}`. But closure reference works.\n\nNeed also consider invalid origin result: `normalizeConfig` throws TypeError; this is inside load chain, caught by catch, entry deleted. API maps TypeError with tenantId? In API, TypeError with /tenantId/ -> 400; other TypeError (origin returned invalid config) -> 500. Good.\n\nNeed API contract: 503 + retry-after:1 for ConfigOriginError. 500 for other origin/config errors. 400 invalid tenant. 405 wrong method. 404 unknown route. JSON no-store. Already okay.\n\nNeed ensure successful entries not evicted merely to make retries pass. Our fix only deletes on rejection. Good.\n\nNeed also check test \"retries the origin after a transient load failure\". With current code, await cache.getConfig('acme') rejects, then second call gets same rejected promise. That test would fail before fix. It is in committed tests. Run tests now maybe see failure. Let's run after maybe.\n\nNeed produce regression evidence in student-tests.mjs at least three meaningful node:test cases covering incident and important behavior that could be broken by overly broad fix. Ideas:\n1. Test that a rejected load is removed and a later read retries (covers incident).\n2. Test that a late failure from an explicitly cleared/replaced entry does not delete the replacement (failure cleanup only matching load).\n3. Test that a successful result is retained across repeated reads, not evicted by retry logic (successful entries not evicted). Or test coalescing on failure for multiple concurrent waiters share failure. Or test normalization of invalid result causes retry (failed/invalid origin result must not poison future reads). Let's add 3 meaningful: \n- \"retries after a rejected origin promise and does not cache the failure\" \n- \"concurrent waiters on a failing load all see the same error, and the next read retries\" \n- \"a fa