{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_d4a4d1a83d0e4c3f6d49b888","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"kimi-k2.7-code","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Need handle potential ESLint issues? The arrow function inside catch using `pending` variable before assignment? In JS let hoisted, assignment occurs before catch handler runs. It's fine. Some lint may complain about `pending` used before defined? Since `pending` is declared and in same block, the closure captures binding; by time catch runs, it's assigned. Static analysis might warn `pending` was used before it was assigned if lint no-use-before-define? ESLint no-use-before-define flags variables used before declared? It is declared before use, but assignment after. Usually no-use-before-define doesn't flag because lexical declaration occurs. Some with `nofunc`. But to avoid risk, could define `let pending = entries.get(key);` then inside if: `const loadPromise = ...; pending = loadPromise.catch(...)`; entries.set. We already do that. The catch references `pending` variable; it is initialized with entries.get (maybe undefined) then assigned. That's okay.\n\nBut maybe some strict linter (no-use-before-define) may consider the closure use as a reference? The variable is declared in the outer function scope and used within a function expression inside its initializer. ESLint no-use-before-define by default checks reference before declaration but not in nested functions? Actually rule `no-use-before-define` checks references in the same scope. The closure is a nested scope, but the reference is not evaluated until later. ESLint's rule may still flag if it treats any reference in initializer as before defined? Let's check not needed? We can test later with `npm run lint`. Let's run tests first, then maybe lint.\n\nNow student-tests. Write file with three tests. Need import CONFIG? Use own. Could import from committed? No. Define local frozen config.\n\nLet's craft tests carefully. Need use `assert.rejects` maybe `assert.deepEqual`. For shared failure, avoid unhandled rejections. Could do:\n\n```\ntest(\"shares a single failure among concurrent waiters and then retries\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  const cache = createConfigCache(async () => {\n    calls += 1;\n    throw new ConfigOriginError(`failure #${calls}`);\n  });\n\n  const first = cache.getConfig(\"acme\").catch((error) => error);\n  const second = cache.getConfig(\"acme\").catch((error) => error);\n  const [err1, err2] = await Promise.all([first, second]);\n\n  assert.equal(err1, err2); // same ConfigOriginError instance\n  assert.equal(err1.message, \"failure #1\");\n  assert.equal(calls, 1);\n\n  await assert.rejects(cache.getConfig(\"acme\"), ConfigOriginError);\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n```\n\nThat works. For first test: no cache on failure and retries.\n\n```\ntest(\"removes a failed load from the cache so the next read retries\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  const cache = createConfigCache(async () => {\n    calls += 1;\n    if (calls === 1) throw new ConfigOriginError(\"timeout\");\n    return CONFIG;\n  });\n  await assert.rejects(cache.getConfig(\"acme\"), ConfigOriginError);\n  assert.equal(cache.size(), 0);\n  assert.deepEqual(await cache.getConfig(\"acme\"), CONFIG);\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n```\n\nThird test: replacement not deleted by late failure.\n\nNeed hold first load with deferred resolve. Let first resolve with error. Then clear and start second, resolve second, then resolve first. Catch first to avoid unhandled.\n\n```\ntest(\"does not let a late failure from a replaced load evict the replacement\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  let finishFirst;\n  let finishSecond;\n  const cache = createConfigCache(() => {\n    calls += 1;\n    if (calls === 1) {\n      return new Promise((resolve) => { finishFirst = resolve; });\n    }\n    return new Promise((resolve) => { finishSecond = resolve; });\n  });\n\n  const firstRequest = cache.getConfig(\"acme\").catch(() => undefined);\n  cache.clear(\"acme\");\n\n  const secondRequest = cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n  finishSecond(CONFIG);\n  assert.deepEqual(await secondRequest, CONFIG);\n  assert.equal(cache.size(), 1);\n\n  finishFirst(new ConfigOriginError(\"late failure\"));\n  await firstRequest;\n\n  const third = await cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n  assert.deepEqual(third, CONFIG);\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n```\n\nBut `finishFirst` assigned as resolve; we resolve with error. For promise, calling resolve with an error object fulfills the promise with the error, not rejects. We need the load promise to reject. So instead use `let rejectFirst;` return new Promise((_, reject) => { rejectFirst = reject; }). Then `rejectFirst(new ConfigOriginError(\"late failure\"))`. Good.\n\nAlso `cache.getConfig` returns a promise that will reject when first load rejects; we catch. Good.\n\nLet's adjust.\n\nPotential issue: After `cache.clear`, `firstRequest` is still awaiting the first pending (which got removed). The catch handler of first load will compare entries.get('acme') to first pending. When we clear, entries.get undefined, so condition false -> no deletion. Then second request sets new pending. Then we resolve second. Then reject first; entries.get is second pending, condition false. Good.\n\nNeed ensure firstRequest doesn't accidentally rethrow after we catch? `firstRequest = cache.getConfig(\"acme\").catch(() => undefined);` So awaiting it yields undefined. Good.\n\nLet's write file.","signature":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":14189,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a57c6a4b-4261-4c82-8f24-11909ca7fa0e","uuid":"ad82d4ee-868f-4012-9415-561adbc63d50","timestamp":"2026-08-23T19:26:46.963Z"}
