{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_368c9d91c2a0d2a56294600e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"kimi-k2.7-code","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No node_modules. So lint not possible without install. The user didn't require lint; only run tests. So final should mention not run lint due no node_modules. But we can run npm install? That requires approval maybe. Not necessary.\n\nNow write INCIDENT.md. Need plain language sections: Summary, User impact, Root cause and evidence, Why logs misleading, Fix and regression coverage, Remaining risk.\n\nWe need describe user impact: tenants that experienced an origin timeout kept getting 503 with retry-after even after origin healthy, so new requests didn't recover until cache was manually cleared or process restarted.\n\nRoot cause: Cache stored the in-flight promise as the entry. When origin rejected (timeout), the rejected promise stayed in the Map. Subsequent reads saw a cache hit and awaited the same rejected promise, reproducing the original failure. Code evidence: `config-cache.mjs` `entries.set(key, pending)` placed the pending promise in the map before it settled, and there was no cleanup on rejection. Log evidence: incident.log line 1 cache=miss status=503, line 2 origin-probe status=200 healthy, line 3 request cfg-782 attempt=2 cache=hit status=503. After origin recovered, request still cache=hit and error=origin_timeout, proving the failure was being replayed from cache, not re-fetched.\n\nWhy logs misleading: The healthy origin probe and repeated `cache=hit` lines could suggest caching logic was working and origin is down. Actually they show the opposite: the origin is healthy (probe 200), and cache hit is for the failed promise, not a successful config. The `cache=hit` logs are misleading because a rejected promise is being treated as a valid cached entry; the probe healthy is evidence that the failure is cached, not upstream.\n\nFix: In `config-cache.mjs`, when a load promise rejects, remove the entry from the map only if that exact in-flight promise is still the current entry. This prevents failed loads from poisoning the cache while allowing replacement entries to survive. Keep successful entries unchanged. Smallest maintainable change. No second cache, no retry loops.\n\nRegression coverage: Student tests: failed load removed and retried; concurrent waiters share failure and next read retries; late failure of replaced load doesn't evict replacement. Plus committed tests cover caching, coalescing, API recovery, tenant separation.\n\nRemaining risk/honest operating limit: The cache still stores a promise, so any unhandled exception from loadFromOrigin other than promise rejection (synchronous throw inside `then` is caught by promise). But the key risk: If the origin fails persistently (not transient), every read triggers a fresh origin request until a success, which is correct per requirements but may increase origin load under persistent failure. Also if origin returns successfully but invalid config (throws TypeError), entry is deleted and next read retries; could cause repeated origin calls for bad config. This is acceptable because invalid result must not be cached. Another risk: The promise-identity guard protects against concurrent replacement, but if a future refactor changes the cached value from a promise to an object, the identity check must be updated; tests catch that.\n\nNeed also mention files changed: config-cache.mjs, student-tests.mjs, INCIDENT.md. config-api.mjs unchanged.\n\nNeed check AGENTS.md: do not commit unless user requests. We won't commit. No external services.\n\nLet's write INCIDENT.md.","signature":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":20393,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a57c6a4b-4261-4c82-8f24-11909ca7fa0e","uuid":"daf06ad1-df70-4462-b700-c244baca84d5","timestamp":"2026-08-23T19:27:18.377Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_368c9d91c2a0d2a56294600e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"kimi-k2.7-code","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"functions.Write:17","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/cs/y_h_9x9j2hj316xhs3mvszd80000gn/T/coding-assistant-benchmark-QCb10M/checkout/benchmark/fixtures/T6-rejected-promise-cache/INCIDENT.md","content":"# Config-read incident\n\n## Summary\n\nFor about three seconds, every config read for tenant `acme` returned HTTP 503\n`origin_timeout`, even though the origin itself was healthy. The first request\ntimed out and its rejected promise was cached, so every later request replayed the\nsame failure instead of retrying the origin.\n\n## User impact\n\nAny tenant that hit an origin timeout would keep seeing `config unavailable`\nresponses until the cached rejection was removed by a manual clear or process\nrestart. No amount of waiting or retrying helped, because the cache treated the\nfailed load as a valid entry.\n\n## Root cause and evidence\n\n`config-cache.mjs` stored the in-flight `Promise` in the cache map before it\nsettled:\n\n```js\npending = Promise.resolve()\n  .then(() => loadFromOrigin(key))\n  .then(normalizeConfig);\nentries.set(key, pending);\n```\n\nWhen `loadFromOrigin` rejected with `ConfigOriginError`, the rejected promise\nstayed in the map. The next `getConfig(\"acme\")` found the same promise,\nawaited it again, and returned the original timeout to the caller. The cache\nwas coalescing failures instead of just coalescing misses.\n\nThe incident log shows this exactly:\n\n- `cfg-781` is a `cache=miss` that fails with `origin_timeout`.\n- The very next line is an origin probe returning `200` in 31 ms, proving the\n  origin recovered quickly.\n- `cfg-782` and `cfg-783` are `cache=hit` with the same `origin_timeout`, even\n  though the origin was healthy.\n\nSo the healthy probe and the `cache=hit` lines together are the evidence: the\nfailure was cached, not upstream.\n\n## Why the logs were misleading\n\nA surface reading of the log suggests two wrong conclusions:\n\n1. **\"The origin is still down.\"** The probe line shows the opposite: the\n   origin responded successfully 400 ms after the first failure.\n2. **\"The cache is doing its job.\"** The `cache=hit` labels look like normal\n   cache reuse, but a rejected promise is not a usable config. Treating it as a\n   hit hid the real bug and made the failure look like a continued origin\n   problem.\n\n## Fix and regression coverage\n\n### Fix\n\n`config-cache.mjs` now attaches a rejection handler to the in-flight load. When\nthat load fails, the handler removes the entry from the cache map, but only if\nthe map still points to the exact same in-flight promise. This means:\n\n- A failed load is not cached, so the next read retries the origin.\n- If the entry is explicitly cleared while the load is running, a late failure\n  from the old load cannot delete the replacement entry.\n- Successful loads are left untouched, so they keep being served as cache hits.\n\nNo second cache, no retry loops, and no sleeps were added.\n\n### Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` adds three tests that would fail with the old code or with\nan overly broad fix:\n\n1. A failed load is removed and the next read retries.\n2. Concurrent waiters share one failure, then the next read retries.\n3. A late failure from a load that was cleared and replaced does not evict the\n   replacement entry.\n\nThese are in addition to the committed tests that already verify successful\nentries are cached, concurrent misses are coalesced, the API recovers after a\nfailure, and tenants stay isolated.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\nBecause a failed or invalid load is intentionally not cached, a tenant that\nconstantly receives bad data from the origin will trigger a fresh origin call on\nevery config read. This is the correct behavior per the contract, but under a\npersistent origin outage it increases origin load compared with a circuit\nbreaker or backoff strategy. The application still has no built-in backoff, so\noperators should monitor origin health and use the existing `cache.clear()` or\ntargeted invalidation to manage load if needed.\n"}}],"usage":{"input_tokens":20393,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a57c6a4b-4261-4c82-8f24-11909ca7fa0e","uuid":"803013c3-49d5-4f38-90a7-7a0f88f21e13","timestamp":"2026-08-23T19:27:18.380Z"}
